aising edge; polling is sparse and dates to August"], "rationale": "Crane's incumbency and money advantage on an R+7 district make him favored, but a Democratic-favorable national environment and Nez's competitive late-summer polling keep this closer than a typical R+7 seat. Markets price Nez around one in four, matching a Lean Republican call.", "watch": "Fresh October polling or a shift in the national House environment could push the race toward Tossup or settle it as Likely R.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Newest usable poll is 55 days old; polling uncertainty inflated.", "Incumbent Eli Crane (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Republican incumbent Eli Crane is favored to win a second term in this northeastern Arizona district, which leans Republican by seven points. He faces Jonathan Nez, a former Navajo Nation president, in a rematch of their 2024 contest. Most professional raters call the race Likely Republican, with a couple rating it Lean Republican and one calling it a Tossup. Prediction markets give Nez roughly a one-in-four chance.\n\nCrane holds a clear financial edge, having raised about twice as much as Nez, though Democratic-aligned outside groups have spent against him. The national environment favors Democrats this year, nearly canceling the district's partisan lean on paper. Limited polling from late summer showed Crane ahead by two to eleven points. A Republican PAC attack ad that featured the wrong Navajo man drew unfavorable attention, but there is little sign it altered the race's fundamentals.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1703, "margin": -5.61}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.2296, "margin": -5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.2331, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.2721, "margin": -4.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.1924, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.2064, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.2601, "margin": -4.5}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.2994,